		<div class="post" id="post-2298156">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2298156)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2298156">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/baby-boo" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6336); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6336&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608973938" alt="Baby Boo" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6336)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/baby-boo" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6336); return false;" >Baby Boo</a></span> <span class="odate time_1432029857 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">19 May 2015 10:04</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2298156">
									

<p>Your comment led me to look over the passage with more detailed care than I might have otherwise, but unless I'm missing something important, there's no sign of Rashaun doing anything other than completely and immediately buying the effect as real.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;well&nbsp;this&nbsp;went&nbsp;on&nbsp;longer&nbsp;than&nbsp;I&nbsp;thought</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;I&nbsp;guess&nbsp;the&nbsp;story's&nbsp;got&nbsp;my&nbsp;attention&nbsp;at&nbsp;least</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>What goes through his head? <em>Cast-iron stomach, spatiotemporal distortion, hyperdimensional portal in her gut</em>. What doesn't? <em>Editing, sleight of hand, camera angles, digital manipulation</em>. He &quot;looks over the rest of the webpage&quot;, but there's no indication that he even looks at her <em>userpage</em> to see what other videos she might have. He doesn't search &quot;crazy maci rope trick&quot; to see if anyone, anywhere, has anything to say about it beyond the comments on that one video page.</p>
<p>He glazes over midway through because he <em>assumes</em> that one middle segment is much like the next, in turn because he <em>assumes</em> that Maci doesn't know how to edit, in turn because he <em>assumes</em> that the teen girl in the video <em>is the sole and entire crew of its production</em>. He doesn't even ask his friend where the link came from, before sending an URGENT message to his superiors!</p>
<p>It's important, though, that I don't necessarily think of this as a flaw in the tale. On the contrary, I think it could be read as an interesting take on the mental effects of working for the Foundation &#8212; that a professional scientist comes to suspect <em>a parallel universe in a young girl's stomach</em> before even considering special effects or stage magic!</p>
<p>I am, however, no-voting on it, because I don't genuinely feel that the story is trading on deliberate irony. It feels, instead, that the author wants us to buy into the video's reality without the effort of falsifying the most obvious and immediate objections &#8212; without even bothering, as it were, to wave a hoop around the levitating lady.</p>
<p>My neutral thumb could be swayed upward either by clearer justification for believing in Maci as a legit POI, or by clearer textual suggestion that Rashaun and his superiors could be jumping at shadows; one way or the other, at least <em>nod</em> toward the elephant in the room with VIDEOS ARE EASY TO FAKE painted on his side.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>In short: it <em>seems</em> as though this piece wants us to come away thinking, &quot;Oh gosh, even if they contain <em>this</em> video, what does YouTube and the internet age portend for the future of containment?&quot; But instead, my gut-level reaction is, &quot;Oh gosh, what does it mean for the Foundation if they've gotten so paranoid as to leap up and ring alarums over <em>shit they see on YouTube</em>?&quot;</p>
<p>Edit: &#8230;or you can skip my whole post, because sirpudding summarized <em>everything I said</em> in just three sentences below. Good heavens, I'm a windbag.</p>

							</div>

								
