		<div class="post" id="post-2298360">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2298360)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2298360">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kholettedrake" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1909832); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1909832&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608973938" alt="KholetteDrake" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1909832)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kholettedrake" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1909832); return false;" >KholetteDrake</a></span> <span class="odate time_1432052433 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">19 May 2015 16:20</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2298360">
									

<p>As others have mentioned, this idea does have a lot of meat that can yet be explored, however, I don't think this work suffers for it. This feels like a snapshot in time, a few minutes where a Foundation agent sees something weird and what he's going to have to do about it. Yes, the video might be fake or it might not. But this isn't about that. This is about the knee-jerk reaction to what he was seeing. The verification and testing can and will come later, but this is that moment when the decision to ignore or investigate has to be made and for that, it's good.</p>
<p>In my reading, the description of the video content didn't seem to imply that tricky camera angles were being used or other editing tricks. The text describes something that an experienced agent sees and deems to be worth investigating. Maybe he's right, maybe he's wrong, but what he saw showed it was at least worth looking in to. It says he notifies his boss and they bring an MTF online. Not all MTFs are armed assault squads. I'm sure there is more than one that are, say, experts in video footage and sorting out faked videos from real anomalies. In any case, I just didn't see the issues that others did. The Foundation operates on a better safe than sorry mentality and has the ability to make a false positive completely disappear from the minds of the people involved, so who cares if this one isn't real. Amnestics for everyone and move on to the next thing.</p>

							</div>

								
					
